The problem is equivalent to finding whether a partition of the array's integers exists such that the sum of the integers in one partition is equal to the sum of the integers in the other partition. To do this, we use a 2D dp table where dp[i][j] represents whether a partition of the first i integers exists such that the sum of the integers in one partition is equal to j. We can then compute the answer as dp[n][sum/2], where n is the length of the array and sum is the sum of all the integers in the array.

The key insight is that if we want to know whether a partition exists where the sum of the integers in one partition is equal to j, we only need to consider the previous i integers, since if the sum of the integers in the other partition is equal to j - num, then the sum of the integers in the first partition is equal to j + num.

The time complexity is O(n\*sum), where n is the length of the input array, and sum is the sum of all the integers in the array. The space complexity is O(sum), since we only need to store the results of the previous i integers.

