As the problem is to find the minimum number of perfect square numbers that sum up to n, we can start with the smallest perfect square number 1, which is lower than n, and find the next perfect square number, 2, which is larger than n. Then, we divide n by 2 and repeat finding the next perfect square number that is larger than n/2. By doing so, we are reducing the search space by half with each step. The recursion ends when the square root of n is equal to the nearest integer. The base case of the recursion is when the square root of n is less than or equal to 1. 

The time complexity of the program is O(log n) due to the binary search technique used to find the perfect square numbers. Space complexity is O(log n) due to the memoization using the hash map. 

